# Detection 02 — Suspicious PowerShell Execution (Encoded Command)

**MITRE ATT&CK:** T1059.001 – Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell
**Log Source:** Sysmon Event ID 1 (Process Creation) / PowerShell Script
Block Logging (Event ID 4104)

## Scenario

Attackers commonly use base64-encoded PowerShell commands to evade simple
string-based detection and download or execute payloads. This detection
looks for the `-EncodedCommand` (or `-enc`) flag.

## Simulate It

Atomic Red Team test case **T1059.001**:

```
Invoke-AtomicTest T1059.001
```

Or manually run a benign encoded command to test detection logic, e.g.:

```powershell
powershell.exe -enc <base64-encoded-benign-command>
```

## Detection Rule

See [`rules/suspicious-powershell.yml`](../rules/suspicious-powershell.yml)
— fires on `powershell.exe` process creation where the command line contains
`-enc` or `-EncodedCommand`.

## Investigation Checklist (fill in with your actual findings)

- [ ] Alert timestamp and triggering rule
- [ ] Parent process (what launched PowerShell — Office app? cmd.exe? browser?)
- [ ] Decoded contents of the base64 command
- [ ] User context the process ran under
- [ ] Any follow-on network connections or file writes from the process
- [ ] Screenshot of the alert and decoded command → save to `screenshots/`

