# Detection 03 — Scheduled Task Persistence

**MITRE ATT&CK:** T1053.005 – Scheduled Task/Job: Scheduled Task
**Log Source:** Windows Security Event Log (Event ID 4698 – Scheduled Task
Created) / Sysmon Event ID 1

## Scenario

Attackers frequently create a scheduled task as a persistence mechanism so
their payload survives reboot or re-executes on a timer. This detection
looks for new scheduled task creation, particularly ones referencing
suspicious paths (temp folders, unusual binaries).

## Simulate It

Atomic Red Team test case **T1053.005**:

```
Invoke-AtomicTest T1053.005
```

Or manually create a benign test task:

```powershell
schtasks /create /tn "TestPersistence" /tr "calc.exe" /sc onlogon
```

## Detection Rule

See [`rules/scheduled-task-persistence.yml`](../rules/scheduled-task-persistence.yml)
— fires on Event ID 4698 (scheduled task created), with additional scoring
if the task action path is in a temp/user-writable directory.

## Investigation Checklist (fill in with your actual findings)

- [ ] Alert timestamp and triggering rule
- [ ] Task name and the exact command/binary it runs
- [ ] User account that created the task
- [ ] Trigger type (on logon, daily, at startup, etc.)
- [ ] Is the referenced binary/path legitimate or suspicious?
- [ ] Screenshot of the alert and task details → save to `screenshots/`

